From 235d259a9588f67418042ef064d7d23592f7b794 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Manoj Sekar <sekar@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 12:42:46 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] prplmesh: Fix Agent crash on radios configured with an
 auto-select channel

beerocks_agent crashed in an unrecoverable respawn loop immediately on
startup whenever any radio's wireless configuration specified an
auto-select channel instead of a static numeric channel. Radios with a
static numeric channel were unaffected.

Root cause: the PHY-indexed WLAN parameter reader in the UCI platform
backend converted the configured channel option to an integer without
first validating that the value was actually numeric. When the channel
option held the auto-select keyword rather than a number, the numeric
conversion raised an unhandled exception. This happened very early in
Agent startup, during platform configuration loading, before any
fronthaul or socket setup has taken place, so the uncaught exception
terminated the process immediately. Because this same startup step runs
on every launch attempt, the Agent crashed again right away on every
respawn, producing a permanent restart loop and leaving the Agent and
all of its dependent fronthaul processes entirely absent from the
system for the remainder of that boot cycle.

This code path was the only one in the codebase missing this handling:
the sibling channel-reading helper used for the non-PHY-indexed lookup
in the same file, and the equivalent helper in the RDK platform
backend, already treat the auto-select keyword (and any other
non-numeric value) as a request for automatic channel selection and
map it to the zero-channel sentinel already used throughout the
codebase to mean "let the driver/hostapd select a channel
automatically," instead of attempting a numeric conversion. The
PHY-indexed variant was added later for the per-PHY radio lookup path
and never received the same safeguard.

Fix: align the PHY-indexed channel reader with the same auto-select
and non-numeric handling already established by its two sibling
implementations, so all three channel-reading paths behave
consistently and none of them can crash on a non-numeric channel value.

This value is only ever used as the one-time startup hint sent to the
fronthaul AP manager, requesting that it run automatic channel
selection when the sentinel value is seen. The actual, post-selection
operating channel continues to be reported back separately and
asynchronously once the driver completes channel selection, and is
stored and consumed by every other part of the Agent exactly as
before; that existing flow is untouched by this change.

Signed-off-by: Manoj Sekar <sekar@qti.qualcomm.com>
---
 framework/platform/bpl/cfg/uci/bpl_cfg_wifi.cpp | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/framework/platform/bpl/cfg/uci/bpl_cfg_wifi.cpp b/framework/platform/bpl/cfg/uci/bpl_cfg_wifi.cpp
index d621e1b..99a433b 100644
--- a/framework/platform/bpl/cfg/uci/bpl_cfg_wifi.cpp
+++ b/framework/platform/bpl/cfg/uci/bpl_cfg_wifi.cpp
@@ -785,6 +785,12 @@ static int cfg_get_wifi_params_from_phy(const std::string &phy_name,
     }
 
     // channel
+    //
+    // The "channel" option may hold "auto" instead of a numeric value, requesting
+    // automatic channel selection. Validate the value is numeric before converting it,
+    // and treat "auto" or any other non-numeric value as a request for automatic
+    // channel selection (represented by channel=0), consistent with how this same
+    // option is handled elsewhere in this module.
     wlan_params->channel = 0;
 
     std::string channel;
@@ -793,7 +799,12 @@ static int cfg_get_wifi_params_from_phy(const std::string &phy_name,
                        radio_section,
                        "channel",
                        channel)) {
-        wlan_params->channel = std::stoi(channel);
+        if (channel == "auto" ||
+            channel.find_first_not_of("0123456789") != std::string::npos) {
+            wlan_params->channel = 0;
+        } else {
+            wlan_params->channel = std::stoi(channel);
+        }
     }
 
     // country code
